Subject: advance the log tail lazily, and durably where it matters

The journal superblock's start/sequence -- the log tail -- was written to disk
on every checkpoint completion, through ext4_block_writebytes, with no barrier
anywhere near it. On a device with a volatile write cache that ordering is
fiction, and the trace of a failing cut showed the consequence measured: the
tail advance dropped at the crash while the log space it freed, already
reused, landed. Recovery started from a stale tail into overwritten records
and stopped having replayed nothing.

Do what jbd2 does instead. Stop writing the superblock on checkpoint
completion -- stage the tail in memory, where every completion already
maintains it. A lagging on-disk tail is safe: recovery replays further back,
which is idempotent redo now that every freed block carries a revoke (previous
patch). A leading tail is what corrupts, and nothing leads any more.

The tail is written durably at exactly the points where the world must agree
with it:

  * the wrap path, where the allocator hands out freed log space after
    forcing a checkpoint: checkpoint, barrier, write the tail, barrier, then
    reuse.
  * the lap guard, which is the subtle one. When checkpoints complete
    promptly the in-memory tail keeps pace with the head and the wrap path
    never fires -- while the tail on the medium goes stale and the head laps
    it. Recovery then reads a superblock naming a position the log has
    overwritten and replays nothing at all; a torn image whose superblock
    said "start 361, sequence 607" while block 361 held sequence 936 is how
    this announced itself. So the journal now remembers the tail it last
    published (published_start), and republishes -- barrier, superblock,
    barrier -- before the head allocates over it. Once per lap of the ring.
  * jbd_journal_stop, already behind the purge, whose flushed checkpoints
    are now barriered before the superblock claims no replay is needed.
  * recovery, which barriers the replay writes before retiring the log.

Cost: the steady state is cheaper -- one fewer 1 KB direct write per
checkpoint completion, no new barriers on the commit path. The wrap and the
lap guard each pay their barriers once per log cycle, which disappears into
any workload big enough to cause one.

Measured with the write-cache model, the Linux kernel replaying every torn
image: the small-journal batch=16 cell went from damaged to 24/24 clean; the
same cuts replayed by this code's own recovery went 16/16; a 54-image sweep
of late cuts -- plain truncation and reordered, batch 1 and 16 -- came back
0 damaged; the barriers-ignored negative control still fails, so the suite
can still say no.
